  "tags": ["circumference", "circles", "real-world", "annulus", "low-floor", "high-ceiling"],
  "preview": "Two neighbors have circular gardens. Mira's has a 4-foot radius. Sam's diameter is 3 feet larger than Mira's diameter. How much border does Sam need?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Use the circumference formula to compute and compare two circles, and reason about how a fixed change in diameter propagates through the formula.",
    "studentPrompt": "Mira and Sam are neighbors. Each has a circular flower garden in their front yard.\n\n- Mira's garden has a radius of 4 feet.\n- Sam's garden has a diameter that is 3 feet larger than Mira's diameter.\n\nUse 3.14 for π. Round to the nearest hundredth.\n\n1. How much landscape border does Sam need to go all the way around her garden?\n2. How much more border does Sam need than Mira?\n3. If Sam's garden's diameter were instead 3 feet *smaller* than Mira's, how much border would Sam need?\n4. The garden store sells border in 5-foot lengths for $7.99 each. How much will Sam spend (using the original diameter from #1)?",
    "image": {
      "alt": "Two circles side by side. Mira's labeled radius 4 ft. Sam's drawn larger with the diameter labeled '11 ft' (= 8 ft + 3 ft).",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" fill=\"none\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"110\" r=\"60\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.18\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"110\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"140\" y=\"104\">4 ft</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"205\">Mira</text></g><circle cx=\"360\" cy=\"110\" r=\"82\" fill=\"#3f42d9\" fill-opacity=\"0.18\"/><line x1=\"278\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"442\" y2=\"110\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"360\" y=\"104\">8 + 3 = 11 ft</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"205\">Sam</text></g></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Show the picture. Tell the story. Don't pre-derive 'circumference equals pi times diameter' — let groups go and recall the formula themselves (or look it up).",
    "materials": ["Vertical whiteboards", "Markers", "Calculator allowed"],
    "concepts": ["circumference", "diameter", "radius", "pi"],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Solid BTC. Multiple parts, real-world. Q3 forces students to reconsider their setup. Q4 brings in cost and discrete units (you can't buy half a 5-foot piece).",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Mira's diameter = 8 ft. Sam's diameter = 8 + 3 = 11 ft. Sam's circumference = π × 11 ≈ 34.54 ft.\n- Q2: Mira's circumference = π × 8 ≈ 25.12 ft. Difference = 34.54 - 25.12 = 9.42 ft. (Or: π × 3 ≈ 9.42 — they could compute the difference directly from the diameter difference!)\n- Q3: Sam's diameter = 5 ft. Circumference = π × 5 ≈ 15.7 ft.\n- Q4: 34.54 / 5 = 6.908 lengths needed → must round UP to 7 lengths. 7 × $7.99 = $55.93.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Using radius in the formula C = πd, getting half the right answer.\n- For Q2, computing the two circumferences separately (correct, but missing the elegant shortcut: diff = π × Δd).\n- For Q4, dividing and rounding *down* (you can't buy a fractional piece — must round up).",
    "discussionQuestions": "- For Q2, two groups got 9.42. One subtracted two circumferences. The other multiplied 3 × π directly. Why do they agree?\n- In Q4, why do we round up instead of down?\n- Suppose Sam's diameter increases by another 3 feet. What does the border need increase by? Is there a pattern?",
    "extensions": "- Sam decides to put a 1-foot-wide stone path *around* her garden. What is the area of just the path? (Annulus.)\n- A third neighbor wants their garden's circumference to be exactly 50 feet. What should the diameter be?\n- Mira's lawn is 20 ft × 30 ft, with her circular garden in the middle. What area of lawn (not garden) needs mowing?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Mira's diameter = 8 ft. Sam's diameter = 11 ft. Sam's circumference = π × 11 ≈ 3.14 × 11 = 34.54 ft.\n2. Mira's circumference = π × 8 ≈ 25.12 ft. Difference = 34.54 - 25.12 = 9.42 ft. (Or directly: π × 3 ≈ 9.42 ft. The difference in circumference equals π times the difference in diameter.)\n3. Sam's smaller diameter = 5 ft. Circumference = π × 5 ≈ 15.7 ft.\n4. Sam needs 34.54 ft. 34.54 / 5 = 6.908 → round up to 7 lengths. 7 × $7.99 = $55.93.",
